### Context
Assume we have the following schema:
```ts
export const TheSchema {
	...,
	models: {
	    SomeModel: {
	        brands: {
	            type: Array,
                    required: true,
	            items: {
                        type: String,
	                enum: Object.values(Brand)
	            }
	        }
	    }
	}
}
```
the following Entity type:
```ts
export type SomeModelEntity = Entity<typeof TheSchema.models.SomeModel>;
```
and the following code:
```ts
const {brands}: SomeModelEntity = {
	...
}
```

### Previously
when we check the type of `brands` we're seeing that it has the type `string[]` instead of the type `Brand[]`

### New
When we check the type of `brands` we'll see that it has type `Brand[]`

1. Created a `usePermissionProxy` function that accepts player, documentType, getter and setter functions. This function allows us to proxy all the permission functionality to `useCharacter` and `useAccount` with the same interface.
    ```typescript
    const { permissions, groupPermissions } = useCharacter(player);
    ```
    ```typescript
    const { permissions, groupPermissions } = useAccount(player); 
    ```
    All permissions/groupPermissions methods are the same for both character and account.
2. Improved docs around account/character permissions and group permissions.
3. Added a function that checks if an entity has any group with one of specified permissions:
    ```typescript
     const groups = {
         admin: ['noclip', 'ban'],
         vip: ['debugcamera']
      }
      const isAvailable = useCharacter(player).groupPermissions.hasAtLeastOneGroupWithSpecificPerm(groups);
      // This will check if character has `admin` group with `noclip` or `ban` permission, or it has `vip` group with `debugcamera` permission.
      ```
4. Added a broadcast function to messenger with the same options, as commands have. This allows us to send message to group of users according to their groups/permissions.
